      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Watermelon and cola are the pair that makes the point. Watermelon has the higher index of
        the two, but a cup of it carries so little carbohydrate that the load lands at 8. Cola has
        a lower index, and 39 g of carbohydrate in the can takes it to 25, above the low band
        despite the milder index. Ranking by index alone would prefer the cola; ranking by load
        does not. When a meal needs both a low index and a low load, the
        <a href="/tools/nutrition-calculator" class="text-[var(--color-wine)] underline">nutrition
        calculator</a> puts the carbohydrate total beside it, and the
        <a href="/tools/dietary-finder" class="text-[var(--color-wine)] underline">dietary meal
        finder</a> handles the low-carb and keto tags if the goal is the load rather than the index.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-display text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-4">
        What the tables do not settle
      </h3>
      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The authors of the international tables are direct about the limits. Many foods have been
        tested only once, in ten or fewer subjects, and values for some products are drifting
        upward as manufacturing favours faster cooking. Porridge oats vary enough that a single
        number can mislead. The same paper notes that the World Health Organization, the American
        Diabetes Association, Diabetes UK and the Canadian Diabetes Association give qualified
        support for the concept while many clinicians still consider glycemic index and load too
        variable for everyday use, and it states plainly that the index should not be used in
        isolation from energy density and macronutrient profile. A revised edition of the tables
        was published in the American Journal of Clinical Nutrition in 2021, which is where the
        current values for most foods now come from.
      </p>

      <p class="text-sm text-[var(--text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        Sources:
        <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2584181/" rel="noopener" class="text-[var(--color-wine)] underline">Atkinson, Foster-Powell and Brand-Miller, &ldquo;International Tables of Glycemic Index and Glycemic Load Values: 2008&rdquo;, <em>Diabetes Care</em> 31(12):2281&ndash;2283</a>;
        <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34258626/" rel="noopener" class="text-[var(--color-wine)] underline">Atkinson et al., &ldquo;International tables of glycemic index and glycemic load values 2021: a systematic review&rdquo;, <em>American Journal of Clinical Nutrition</em></a>.
      </p>
